Here is the real kernel after repack, in RVV intrinsics (VLEN=1024 version: N=64 rows, so int16 = m1, fp32 = m2). This is section 5.3's pseudo code made concrete - illustration for F4b, not yet built/tested:

```c
// Computes 64 outputs at once: s[j] = dot(weight row j, y),  j = 0..63
// n = K (columns), nb = n/128 blocks.
//
// Repacked weight layout per 128-col block (made once by repack_q1_0_to_q1_0_Nx1):
//   ggml_half d[64];        // one scale per row            (128 bytes)
//   uint8_t   qs[128][8];   // column c -> its 64 sign bits (8 bytes per column)

void gemv_q1_0_64x1_q8_0(int n, float * s, const void * vx, const block_q8_0 * y) {
    const int nb = n / 128;
    const uint8_t * p = (const uint8_t *) vx;   // walks the repacked bytes in order
    const size_t vl = 64;                       // N = 64 lanes, always full

    // lane j = row j's final answer (like the last line of the toy example)
    vfloat32m2_t sumf = __riscv_vfmv_v_f_f32m2(0.0f, vl);

    for (int ib = 0; ib < nb; ib++) {
        // 64 per-row weight scales: fp16 -> fp32, into 64 lanes
        vfloat16m1_t dxh = __riscv_vle16_v_f16m1((const _Float16 *) p, vl);
        vfloat32m2_t dx  = __riscv_vfwcvt_f_f_v_f32m2(dxh, vl);
        p += 64 * 2;

        vfloat32m2_t facc = __riscv_vfmv_v_f_f32m2(0.0f, vl);  // this block's dots

        for (int g = 0; g < 4; g++) {                  // 4 q8_0 groups of 32 cols
            const block_q8_0 * yb = &y[4*ib + g];
            const float dy = GGML_CPU_FP16_TO_FP32(yb->d);   // scalar, shared by rows

            vint16m1_t sumi = __riscv_vmv_v_x_i16m1(0, vl);  // 64 running sums

            for (int c = 0; c < 32; c++) {
                const int16_t yc = yb->qs[c];          // ONE activation (scalar)

                // column c's 64 sign bits -> mask (this is why we repacked)
                vbool16_t sign = __riscv_vlm_v_b16(p, vl);
                p += 8;                                // 64 bits = 8 bytes

                sumi = __riscv_vsub_vx_i16m1(sumi, yc, vl);              // all: -y
                sumi = __riscv_vadd_vx_i16m1_mu(sign, sumi, sumi, 2*yc, vl); // bit=1: +2y
            }
            // net: bit=1 lane got -y+2y = +y, bit=0 lane got -y. The sign trick.

            // facc += dy * sumi   (64 lanes at once; dy stays scalar, no accuracy loss)
            facc = __riscv_vfmacc_vf_f32m2(facc,
                       dy, __riscv_vfwcvt_f_x_v_f32m2(sumi, vl), vl);
        }

        sumf = __riscv_vfmacc_vv_f32m2(sumf, dx, facc, vl);  // apply row scales
    }

    __riscv_vse32_v_f32m2(s, sumf, vl);   // store 64 finished outputs. Done.
}
```

Map it to the toy example from before:

| toy example step | intrinsic |
|---|---|
| "signs of column c: (+,-,+,-)" | `vlm_v_b16` - one mask load, 64 bits |
| "each lane adds +y or -y" | `vsub_vx` then masked `vadd_vx` (+2y) |
| "last line = all answers done" | `sumf`, stored once with `vse32` |

Now compare with the old kernel's problems:

- **No `vwredsum` anywhere.** Search the code - it is not there. The K-sum lives inside `sumi`'s lanes, one per row. That was the whole point of section 5.
- **No `vmv.x.s`, no scalar FMA.** Nothing ever crosses vector -> scalar. The scalar reads (`yb->qs[c]`, `yb->d`) go scalar -> vector only, and they do not depend on any vector result, so the CPU loads them ahead while the vector unit works. No waiting.
- **All 64 lanes always useful.** `vl` is 64 every instruction. Inner loop = 3 ops (`vlm` + `vsub` + `vadd`) that each do 64 rows of work.

LMUL-adaptive part: on VLEN=512 (premium) the same structure uses `i16m2`/`f32m4` types (`vbool8_t` mask); on VLEN=2048, `i16mf2`... in practice you select the type set from `vlenb` at runtime via the dispatcher, exactly like `_vl256`/`_vl128` today. Structure identical, only the type suffixes change.

